                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_n(X,x) := \pi_n(|X_\bullet|,x)
                    \\,.
          %p
            In this form the definition originates in (
            %a{:href => "#Segal"}> Segal
            ).
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
            under the natural embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LieGrpd
                    %mo ↪
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi LieGrpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LieGrpd \hookrightarrow \infty LieGrpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9ELieGrpd"} ∞LieGrpd
            this is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} locally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            comes with a further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Π
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi LieGrpd
                  %mo →
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Pi \dashv \Delta \dashv \Gamma) : \infty LieGrpd \to \infty Grpd
                  \\,.
          %p
            We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X) \in \infty Grpd \simeq Top
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid+in+a+locally+%E2%88%9E-connected+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} fundamental ∞-groupoid in a locally ∞-connected (∞,1)-topos
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .un_prop
            %h6#observation Observation
            %p
              The geometric homotopy groups of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              are those of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X) \in Top
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By the discussion at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
              we have precisely that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
              is presented by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+topological+space"} simplicial topological space
              underlying the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h2#references References
          %p The definition of the homotopy groups of a Lie groupoid as those of its geometric realization appearently goes back to
          %ul#Segal
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              %em Classifying spaces and spectral sequences
              , IHES Publ. Math. 34 (1968) 105–112.
          %p An equivalent definition is in
          %ul
            %li A. Haefliger, Groupoïdes d’holonomie et espaces classiants , Astérisque 116 (1984), 70-97
          %p reproduced in section 3 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              %em Classifying spaces related to foliations
              , Topology 17 (1978), 367-382.
